        content: "International efforts to limit the nuclear threat. Partial Nuclear Test Ban Treaty (1963): ended atmospheric nuclear testing following public health concerns about radioactive fallout. Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT, 1968): countries with nuclear weapons agreed not to help others acquire them; non-nuclear countries agreed not to develop them; all agreed to work toward disarmament. The NPT is the most important nuclear governance instrument — 191 signatories. The problem: Israel, India, Pakistan, and North Korea never signed or withdrew; they all developed nuclear weapons anyway. Strategic Arms Reduction Treaties (START I, II, New START): US-Soviet/Russian agreements to reduce strategic nuclear warheads — producing significant reductions from Cold War peaks. New START expires in 2026 with no current replacement agreed. The current landscape: nine nuclear-armed states; increased tensions between the US and Russia; North Korea expanding its arsenal; Iran's nuclear program; China's significant buildup. The fundamental question: has nuclear deterrence prevented large-scale war since 1945, or have we simply been lucky?"
